# THE FLEET — every server the Swarm runs on. This file is the source of truth.
#
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# THE TABLE
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# One line per node, six whitespace-separated columns:
#
#     hostname   site   role   ssh_user   public_ipv4   labels
#
#   site       which cluster this machine belongs to. A site is a set of
#              machines close enough to share a Swarm — Raft wants
#              single-digit milliseconds. `hetzner-de` and `azure-in` are
#              130 ms apart and are therefore SEPARATE SWARMS, federated at
#              the storage and DNS layers, never stretched into one.
#   role       manager = Raft voter; worker = runs containers only. Keep
#              managers ODD **per site**. See "CHOOSING A ROLE" below.
#   ssh_user   the account CI and operators log in as. It differs per machine
#              because they were built by different people at different times.
#   labels     comma-separated key=value applied to the Swarm node, or `-`
#              for none. THIS IS THE IMPORTANT COLUMN — see below.
#
# ─── ADDRESS ROLES, NOT MACHINES ───────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The fleet is heterogeneous and temporary. Providers change, addresses change,
# Hetzner will be retired entirely. So nothing may be pinned to a machine. A
# service declares what KIND of node it needs:
#
#     constraints: [node.labels.node_role == edge]    # good
#     constraints: [node.hostname == rishi-4]         # forbidden
#
# Replace a dead box, give the new one the old one's labels, and every service
# follows it automatically. That is the whole trick, and the labels column is
# where those labels are declared. `scripts/ci/apply_fleet_labels.py`
# reconciles the live Swarm to what is written here.
#
# Addresses work the same way: services inside a Swarm find each other by NAME
# over overlay DNS, never by IP. Proven 2026-09-25 — a storage node was
# restarted onto a different overlay IP mid-test and the cluster never noticed.
# The only place an address belongs is the table below.
#
# NOT LISTED YET: the first Azure Central India box, 98.70.42.115. It is
# provisioned and reachable (user `rishi-machine-access`), but its hostname is
# `rishi-1`, which COLLIDES with the Hetzner rishi-1, and it is not in a Swarm.
# It joins this table once renamed and clustered — the table is what the fleet
# IS, not what has been ordered.
#
# ADDING A SERVER IS ADDING A LINE HERE — then follow the checklist below.
# `scripts/ci/check_fleet_inventory.py` fails CI if any address named under
# .github/workflows, scripts/ or bootstrap/ is missing from this table, and
# the nightly "Fleet drift" workflow fails if the live Swarm stops matching it.

FLEET_NODES="
rishi-1     hetzner-de  worker   deploy                138.201.137.181  -
rishi-2     hetzner-de  worker   deploy                136.243.150.84   -
rishi-3     hetzner-de  worker   deploy                136.243.147.225  -
rishi-4     hetzner-de  manager  rishi-deploy          138.201.128.108  node_role=edge,state_tier=primary
rishi-5     hetzner-de  manager  rishi-deploy          88.99.160.251    node_role=edge,observability_tier=primary
rishi-6     hetzner-de  manager  rishi-deploy          162.55.88.112    node_role=compute,langfuse_tier=primary
"

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# CHOOSING A ROLE — target is 5 managers, everything else a worker
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# Managers vote. A Swarm survives losing (managers-1)/2 of them, so 3 tolerates
# 1 loss and 5 tolerates 2. Keep the count ODD — 4 managers tolerate exactly as
# many failures as 3 do, so the fourth buys nothing. Past 7 the Raft chatter
# costs more than the resilience is worth.
#
# Today all three managers (rishi-4/5/6) are ALSO the three Patroni database
# nodes. Losing two of those boxes therefore takes out Postgres and the Swarm's
# ability to decide anything, in one event. So the next two managers should be
# nodes that do NOT run Patroni. Everything after that joins as a worker —
# workers are free, they never affect quorum.
#
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# CHECKLIST — a node in this table is not yet a node doing work
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# Joining the Swarm is one step of six. Four of them live outside this repo,
# and skipping them gives you a node that is "in the cluster" and idle.
#
#   1. SSH — our CI key must be on the box. Access is governed by
#      `yral-current-team-ssh-keys.md` in dolr-ai/yral, and Ansible rewrites
#      authorized_keys from that file every Monday. A key added by hand
#      disappears at the next run. Add it there, by PR.
#
#   2. FIREWALL — Swarm needs 2377/tcp (management), 7946/tcp+udp (node
#      discovery) and 4789/udp (overlay traffic) open between every pair of
#      nodes. ufw is per-node, set by bootstrap/scripts/node-bootstrap.sh, and
#      is NOT managed by Ansible — so rules added here persist, and rules
#      forgotten here stay forgotten.
#
#   3. JOIN — `docker swarm join` with the manager or worker token from a
#      current manager, then promote/demote to match the role column.
#
#   4. LABELS — an unlabelled node cannot run the edge. The edge Caddy is
#      constrained to `node.labels.node_role == edge`; rishi-1/2/3 carry no
#      labels at all today, which is why they never serve TLS. Label a new
#      node deliberately or it will only ever run unconstrained services.
#
#   5. DNS — `*.rishi.yral.com` is a fixed set of A records, one per node, in
#      the GOBAZZINGA Cloudflare account. A new box receives no traffic until
#      its address is added there. We cannot make this change ourselves.
#
#   6. CAPACITY — more nodes is not more capacity. `yral-rishi-agent` runs
#      `replicas=2`; raise it or the new hardware sits idle.
#
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# WHAT MUST NEVER BECOME DYNAMIC
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# The stateful services are pinned to named hostnames ON PURPOSE:
#
#     patroni-stack.yml        etcd-1/2/3  → rishi-4 / rishi-5 / rishi-6
#     redis-sentinel-stack.yml all services → rishi-4 / rishi-5 / rishi-6
#     langfuse-stack.yml       all services → rishi-6
#
# They hold local disk state and are quorum members of their own clusters.
# Rescheduling them is not a rebalance, it is data loss. Do not "improve" those
# constraints into labels or spread policies. Only the stateless tier — the
# agent app, amorae-web, the ComfyUI tunnel — should expand onto new nodes.
#
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# REMOVING A NODE — deleting a line does NOT drain a server
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# Nothing automatically removes a node when its line disappears, and that is
# deliberate. A reconciler that faithfully applied a deletion would drain a
# production box on a bad merge or a typo — and on a manager, that can cost
# quorum. Removal stays a deliberate, separately confirmed act:
#
#   docker node update --availability drain <hostname>   # move work off it
#   ...confirm services rescheduled...
#   docker node demote <hostname>                        # if it is a manager
#   docker node rm <hostname>
#
# Delete the line here only AFTER the node is out. Until then the nightly
# drift check will correctly report the mismatch, which is the point.

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# DERIVED VIEWS — nothing below needs editing when a node is added or removed
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

fleet_column() {
    # fleet_column <column-number> [role] [site]
    echo "${FLEET_NODES}" | awk -v col="$1" -v want="${2:-}" -v site="${3:-}" \
        'NF == 6 && (want == "" || $3 == want) && (site == "" || $2 == site) { print $col }'
}

FLEET_SITES="$(fleet_column 2 | sort -u | tr '\n' ' ')"
FLEET_HOSTNAMES="$(fleet_column 1 | tr '\n' ' ')"
FLEET_IPS="$(fleet_column 5 | tr '\n' ' ')"

# The site the production Swarm lives on today. Every existing caller that asks
# for "the managers" means THIS site's managers — a second site has its own, and
# mixing them is how a command lands on the wrong cluster.
FLEET_DEFAULT_SITE=hetzner-de
FLEET_MANAGER_IPS="$(fleet_column 5 manager "${FLEET_DEFAULT_SITE}" | tr '\n' ' ')"
FLEET_WORKER_IPS="$(fleet_column 5 worker "${FLEET_DEFAULT_SITE}" | tr '\n' ' ')"

# The SSH account on the managers, which is where CI connects. Workers are
# reached through the Swarm, not directly, so CI needs only this one.
SWARM_USER=rishi-deploy
